diff --git a/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000000..5515c614b25
--- /dev/null
+++ b/bsps/powerpc/ibookg3/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+ibookg3
+=======
+
+BSP for the Apple iBook G3 (PowerPC 750 family, NewWorld Open Firmware,
+UniNorth host bridge, KeyLargo mac-io). It is developed against QEMU's
+`mac99` machine with `-cpu 750cx`, which models this hardware.
+
+What it uses:
+
+* Open Firmware client interface at boot, to read the CPU, bus and timebase
+  frequencies, the RAM size and the mac-io base address from the device tree
+* KeyLargo ESCC channel A (Z85C30) as a polled console
+* decrementer as the clock, timebase as the CPU counter
+* KeyLargo OpenPIC for external interrupts, with the generic interrupt
+  controller API (enable, disable, priorities, pending state); handlers may
+  be preempted by interrupts of higher priority
+* BATs: RAM cached, the mac-io/PCI space at `0x80000000` uncached and guarded
+
+Memory layout: the image is linked to run at 16 MiB (`0x01000000`) with load
+addresses starting at zero, which is what QEMU's `mac99` `-kernel` loader
+expects. The work area runs from the end of the image to the end of RAM as
+reported by Open Firmware. The low 16 MiB holds the exception vectors and is
+otherwise unused.
+
+Interrupt vectors
+-----------------
+
+* 0 to 63: OpenPIC sources
+* 64 to 67: OpenPIC inter-processor interrupts sent to this processor; these
+  are software interrupts which `rtems_interrupt_raise()` can trigger (Time
+  Test 27 and the validation tests use the first two)
+* 68: decrementer
+
+Running on QEMU
+---------------
+
+```shell
+qemu-system-ppc -M mac99 -cpu 750cx -m 128 -nographic -kernel app.exe
+```
+
+The console is the first serial port. After shutdown the BSP idles; it does
+not power off the machine.
+
+For the test suite use `-icount shift=4,align=off` (as `rtems-test` does for
+QEMU): the timer interrupts then follow the instruction count, which the
+timing sensitive tests need. With rtems-tools, use the `ibookg3-qemu` BSP
+configuration of the tester.
+
+Limitations
+-----------
+
+* Only tested on QEMU. Booting on real hardware needs a loader path from
+  Open Firmware (for example from disk) that places the image at 16 MiB.
+* No framebuffer console; the real iBook has no external serial port.
+* No PMU support (power off, real-time clock, battery).
+* No drivers for the other KeyLargo or PCI devices.
